<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Some Review for Exam 2</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The probability density function
        <m>p(t) = \frac{1}{3}e^{-t/3}</m>
        describes the amount of time, in minutes,
        you wait to talk to a representative when
        you call your bank.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the cumulative distribution
          function.  What does this function
          tell us?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="38">
        <p>
          <md>
            C(s) = \int_0^s \frac{1}{3}e^{-t/3}
            \,dt = 1 - e^{-s/3}.
          </md>
          This gives the probability that you wait less
          than <m>s</m> minutes.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the median waiting time.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="38">
        <p>
          Setting <m>C(m) = 0.5</m> gives the expression
          <m>e^{-m/3} = \frac{1}{2}</m>, so
          <m>m = 3\ln(\frac12) \approx 2.08</m> minutes.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the average waiting time.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="38">
        <p>
          Integrating by parts with
          <m>u = t</m> and
          <m>dv = \frac{1}{3}e^{-t/3}\,dt</m>, we see that
          <md>
            \int_0^\infty t\cdot\frac{1}{3}
            e^{-t/3}\,dt
            = \Bigl[-te^{-t/3}\Bigr]_0^\infty
            + \int_0^\infty e^{-t/3}\,dt
            = 0 + 3 = 3
          </md>
          minutes.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What percentage of people wait less
          time than the average?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="38">
        <p>
          <m>C(3) = 1 - e^{-1} \approx 0.632</m>,
          so about <m>63.2\%</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What percentage of people wait less
          time than the median?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="38">
        <p>
          Exactly <m>50\%</m>, which is the definition of
          the median.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Students are given a three-hour test and
        you record the length of time it takes
        students to complete the test.  The
        cumulative distribution function is
        <m>C(t) = t^3/27</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the median time it takes for
          students to complete the test?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="38">
        <p>
          Setting <m>t^3/27 = 0.5</m>:
          <m>t^3 = 13.5</m>, so
          <m>t = \sqrt[3]{13.5} \approx
          2.38</m> hours.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If you finish in 2 hours, what
          percentage of students have finished
          before you?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="38">
        <p>
          <m>C(2) = 8/27 \approx 0.296</m>,
          so about <m>29.6\%</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the probability density
          function <m>p(t)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="38">
        <p>
          <m>p(t) = C'(t) = t^2/9</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The first quartile is the time at
          which 25% of students have finished
          the exam, and the third quartile is
          when 75% have.  Find these times.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="38">
        <p>
          First quartile: <m>t^3/27 = 0.25</m>
          gives <m>t = 3/\sqrt[3]{4}
          \approx 1.89</m> hours.
        </p>
        <p>
          Third quartile: <m>t^3/27 = 0.75</m>
          gives <m>t = \sqrt[3]{81/4}
          \approx 2.73</m> hours.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the average time it takes for
          students to finish the exam.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="38">
        <p>
          <md>
            \int_0^3 t\cdot\frac{t^2}{9}\,dt
            = \int_0^3\frac{t^3}{9}\,dt
            = \left[\frac{t^4}{36}\right]_0^3
            = \frac{81}{36} = \frac{9}{4}
            = 2.25
          </md>
          hours.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the differential equation
        <md>
          \frac{dy}{dt} = 4y - y^3.
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch <m>dy/dt</m> as a function of <m>y</m> and a slope
          field for this equation, and 
          identify any equilibrium solutions.
          For each equilibrium, determine whether
          it is stable or unstable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="38">
        <image width="50%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/38-graph.xml"/>
        </image>
        <image width="50%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/38-slope.xml"/>
        </image>
        
        <p>
          There are stable equilibria at <m>y = -2</m> and <m>y=2</m>
          and an unstable equilibrium at <m>y=0</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>y(0) = 1</m>, use Euler's method
          with a step size of 0.1 to estimate
          the value <m>y(0.4)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="38">
        <p>
          <tabular>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center"/>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell><m>t</m></cell>
              <cell><m>y</m></cell>
              <cell><m>dy/dt</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\Delta y</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>0.0</cell>
              <cell>1.0000</cell>
              <cell>3.000</cell>
              <cell>0.300</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>0.1</cell>
              <cell>1.3000</cell>
              <cell>3.003</cell>
              <cell>0.300</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>0.2</cell>
              <cell>1.6003</cell>
              <cell>2.303</cell>
              <cell>0.230</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>0.3</cell>
              <cell>1.8306</cell>
              <cell>1.188</cell>
              <cell>0.119</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>0.4</cell>
              <cell>1.9494</cell>
              <cell></cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </p>
        <p>
          So <m>y(0.4) \approx 1.95</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Find the solution to the following
        initial value problems.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>\frac{dy}{dt} \amp= 6-y</mrow>
            <mrow>y(0) \amp= 3</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="38">
        <p>
          Separating variables and antidifferentiating shows that
          <m>y = 6 + Ae^{-t}</m>.
          Using <m>y(0) = 3</m>, we see that <m>A = -3</m>,
          so <m>y = 6 - 3e^{-t}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>\frac{dy}{dt} \amp= \frac{1}{y}</mrow>
            <mrow>y(0) \amp= 2</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="38">
        <p>
          Separating variables gives
          <m>y^2 = 2t + C</m>.
          Using <m>y(0) = 2</m>: <m>C = 4</m>,
          so <m>y = \sqrt{2t+4}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Newton's Law of Cooling states that the
        temperature of an object changes at a rate
        proportional to the difference between its
        temperature and room temperature.  Suppose
        that <m>T(t)</m> is the temperature of some
        object sitting in a <m>70^\circ</m> room.
        Newton's Law says that
        <md>
          \frac{dT}{dt} = -k(T-70).
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose that the temperature is initially
        <m>120^\circ</m>.  Ten minutes later, it is
        <m>100^\circ</m>.  When will the temperature
        reach <m>80^\circ</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="38">
      <p>
        The general solution is
        <m>T = 70 + Ae^{-kt}</m>.  Using
        <m>T(0) = 120</m>, we see that <m>A = 50</m>.
        Using <m>T(10) = 100</m> allows us to find <m>k</m> since
        <m>e^{-10k} = \frac{3}{5}</m>, so
        <m>k = \frac{\ln(5/3)}{10}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Setting <m>T = 80</m>:
        <m>e^{-kt} = \frac{1}{5}</m>, giving
        <md>
          t = \frac{\ln 5}{k}
          = \frac{10\ln 5}{\ln(5/3)}
          \approx 31.5
        </md>
        minutes.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
